/**
\page reportsM Reports Menu

The <b>Reports</b> Menu shows commands that generate a read-only report about
the current layout (\ref mainW). Each report opens in its own window with
<b>Save...</b>, <b>Print...</b> and <b>Page Setup...</b> buttons, the same as
the Parts List (\ref cmdEnum) window.

- <b>Unconnected Endpoints</b> - displays the <b>Unconnected Endpoints
  Report</b> (\ref cmdReports) dialog, listing every track endpoint in the
  layout that isn't connected to another track.

- <b>Turnout Density</b> - displays the <b>Turnout Density Report</b>
  (\ref cmdReportsturnout) dialog, listing turnout counts and track length
  by layer, and flagging switching-heavy layers.

- <b>Track Lengths</b> - displays the <b>Track Lengths Report</b>
  (\ref cmdReportstracklen) dialog, listing total track length, turnout
  count, and estimated car capacity by layer.

- <b>Curve Stats</b> - displays the <b>Curve Stats Report</b>
  (\ref cmdReportscurvestats) dialog, listing the layout's curve count,
  radius range, and a radius-distribution histogram.

- <b>Equipment Suitability</b> - displays the <b>Equipment Suitability
  Report</b> (\ref cmdReportsequipment) dialog, rating common equipment
  classes (freight cars, locomotives, passenger cars) against the
  layout's minimum usable curve radius.

- <b>Gaps</b> - displays the <b>Gaps Report</b> (\ref cmdReportsgaps)
  dialog, listing pairs of open track endpoints that are close enough to
  each other that they likely should be connected.

- <b>Kinked Joints</b> - displays the <b>Kinked Joints Report</b>
  (\ref cmdReportskinked) dialog, listing already-connected track joints
  whose alignment has drifted out of tolerance.

- \subpage cmdReports

- \subpage cmdReportsturnout

- \subpage cmdReportstracklen

- \subpage cmdReportscurvestats

- \subpage cmdReportsequipment

- \subpage cmdReportsgaps

- \subpage cmdReportskinked

*/

/**
\page cmdReports Unconnected Endpoints Report

The <b>Unconnected Endpoints Report</b> command lists every track endpoint
in the entire layout that is not connected to another track -- one row per
open endpoint, giving its <b>Track</b> id, the layer it's on (<b>Layer
#</b>/<b>Layer Name</b>), its <b>X</b>/<b>Y</b> position, <b>Angle</b>, and
whether it's <b>Nearby</b> another open endpoint. The layer number matches
the main window's own layer buttons and layer selector (both 1-based) --
useful for finding the right physical level to look at on a multi-level
layout.

![](reportuncon.png)

A summary line above the list gives the total count of open endpoints and how
many are flagged <b>Nearby</b>, updated whenever the report is generated or
refreshed.

Unlike the Parts List (\ref cmdEnum) command, this report always covers the
whole layout; it does not consider the current selection (\ref cmdSelect).

A track with more than two endpoints (for example a turntable) can
contribute more than one row to the report -- one row per unconnected
endpoint on that track, not one row per track.

The <b>Nearby</b> column flags an endpoint when another open endpoint, on a
<i>different</i> track, lies within the layout's Connection Distance
preference (Options > \ref cmdPref) -- the same distance the editor
itself uses to decide whether two endpoints are close enough to connect.
A flagged pair usually means a missed connection rather than a deliberate
dead end. Flagged rows are listed first, grouped together; every row stays
in the list either way, nothing is filtered out.

Selecting a row in the list pans the <b>Main Canvas</b> (\ref mainW) to
that endpoint's position and draws a temporary open-circle indicator there,
so you can quickly find any listed endpoint on the layout. The indicator
is purely visual -- it's never saved with the layout and disappears as
soon as the report window closes. Its color can be changed on the
<b>Colors</b> (\ref cmdRgbcolor) dialog, under <b>Report Indicator</b>.

The <b>Refresh</b> button recomputes the report in place -- useful after
fixing a flagged endpoint elsewhere in the layout, without closing and
reopening the report window.

The <b>Save As...</b> button opens a <i>File Save</i> dialog window that's
used to name and save the report to a text file.

The <b>Print</b> button opens the <i>Print</i> dialog which is used to
print the report.

The <b>Done</b> button closes the <b>Unconnected Endpoints Report</b>
window.

*/

/**
\page cmdReportsturnout Turnout Density Report

The <b>Turnout Density Report</b> command lists, for each layer with any
track on it, the total track length in feet, the turnout count, and the
turnout density (turnouts per 100ft of track) -- one row per layer, sorted
by density, highest first. A layer whose density exceeds 10 turnouts per
100ft is flagged <b>HEAVY</b> (a common sign of a staging yard or other
switching-heavy area).

![](reportturnout.png)

A summary line above the list gives the layout's total turnout count,
total track length, and overall density, updated whenever the report is
generated or refreshed.

Unlike the Parts List (\ref cmdEnum) command, this report always covers the
whole layout; it does not consider the current selection (\ref cmdSelect).

Any turnout with fewer than 3 endpoints (possibly broken or incomplete) is
listed separately in the <b>Save...</b>/<b>Print</b> output, under
<b>PARTIAL TURNOUTS</b>; the summary line notes when any exist. Unlike the
Unconnected Endpoints Report (\ref cmdReports), this report has no
click-to-navigate -- it's a layout-wide statistic, not a list of specific
locations to visit.

The <b>Refresh</b> button recomputes the report in place -- useful after
changing turnouts or track elsewhere in the layout, without closing and
reopening the report window.

The <b>Save As...</b> button opens a <i>File Save</i> dialog window that's
used to name and save the report to a text file.

The <b>Print</b> button opens the <i>Print</i> dialog which is used to
print the report.

The <b>Done</b> button closes the <b>Turnout Density Report</b> window.

*/

/**
\page cmdReportstracklen Track Lengths Report

The <b>Track Lengths Report</b> command lists, for each layer with any
track on it, the total track length in feet, the turnout count, and the
estimated <b>car capacity</b> -- how many average freight cars (~44ft
including couplers, Joe Fugate's operations-planning methodology, MRH Oct
2014) would fit end-to-end along that layer's track, scaled to the
layout's own scale.

![](reporttracklen.png)

A summary line above the list gives the layout's total track length,
turnout count, and total estimated car capacity, updated whenever the
report is generated or refreshed.

Unlike the Parts List (\ref cmdEnum) command, this report always covers the
whole layout; it does not consider the current selection (\ref cmdSelect).
Like the Turnout Density Report (\ref cmdReportsturnout), this report has
no click-to-navigate -- it's a layout-wide statistic, not a list of
specific locations to visit.

The <b>Refresh</b> button recomputes the report in place -- useful after
changing track elsewhere in the layout, without closing and reopening the
report window.

The <b>Save As...</b> button opens a <i>File Save</i> dialog window that's
used to name and save the report to a text file.

The <b>Print</b> button opens the <i>Print</i> dialog which is used to
print the report.

The <b>Done</b> button closes the <b>Track Lengths Report</b> window.

*/

/**
\page cmdReportscurvestats Curve Stats Report

The <b>Curve Stats Report</b> command summarizes every curved track's
radius across the whole layout: total curve count, the minimum, maximum,
and mean radius, and a histogram bucketing every curve into one of five
radius ranges (under 12in, 12-18in, 18-24in, 24-36in, and over 36in,
model-scale inches). The minimum radius is the single most useful number
here -- it determines which locomotives and passenger cars will operate
reliably on the layout.

![](reportcurve.png)

A summary line above the list gives the curve count and radius range,
updated whenever the report is generated or refreshed.

Unlike the Parts List (\ref cmdEnum) command, this report always covers the
whole layout; it does not consider the current selection (\ref cmdSelect).
Like the Turnout Density Report (\ref cmdReportsturnout), this report has
no click-to-navigate -- it's a layout-wide statistic, not a list of
specific locations to visit.

The <b>Refresh</b> button recomputes the report in place -- useful after
changing curves elsewhere in the layout, without closing and reopening the
report window.

The <b>Save As...</b> button opens a <i>File Save</i> dialog window that's
used to name and save the report to a text file.

The <b>Print</b> button opens the <i>Print</i> dialog which is used to
print the report.

The <b>Done</b> button closes the <b>Curve Stats Report</b> window.

*/

/**
\page cmdReportsequipment Equipment Suitability Report

The <b>Equipment Suitability Report</b> command rates a fixed list of
common equipment classes (short/standard/long freight cars, 4- and
6-axle diesels, small/medium/large/articulated steam locomotives, and
standard/long passenger cars) against the layout's minimum usable curve
radius -- the tightest curve any piece of equipment must be able to
negotiate to run anywhere on the layout. Each class shows <b>PASS</b>
(comfortably fits), <b>MARGINAL</b> (within 2 scale inches of the
minimum -- may bind or derail on some equipment), or <b>FAIL</b> (won't
reliably negotiate the tightest curve).

Curves under 9 scale inches are excluded from the minimum-radius scan as
likely decorative (for example a small-radius spur into a scenic detail,
not something rolling stock actually needs to run through at speed).

A summary line above the list gives the layout's minimum usable curve
radius and how many equipment classes were rated, updated whenever the
report is generated or refreshed. If the layout has no curves at or
above the 9in decorative-curve cutoff, the summary and list both say so
instead.

Unlike the Parts List (\ref cmdEnum) command, this report always covers the
whole layout; it does not consider the current selection (\ref cmdSelect).
Like the other phase-2 reports (\ref cmdReportsturnout,
\ref cmdReportstracklen, \ref cmdReportscurvestats), this report has no
click-to-navigate -- it's a layout-wide statistic, not a list of specific
locations to visit.

The <b>Refresh</b> button recomputes the report in place -- useful after
changing curves elsewhere in the layout, without closing and reopening the
report window.

The <b>Save As...</b> button opens a <i>File Save</i> dialog window that's
used to name and save the report to a text file. The saved/printed text
groups equipment classes under PASS/MARGINAL/FAIL headings, unlike the
interactive list above (which keeps the fixed equipment-class order).

The <b>Print</b> button opens the <i>Print</i> dialog which is used to
print the report.

The <b>Done</b> button closes the <b>Equipment Suitability Report</b>
window.

*/

/**
\page cmdReportsgaps Gaps Report

The <b>Gaps Report</b> command lists pairs of open (unconnected) track
endpoints, on two different tracks, that lie within the layout's
Connection Distance preference (Options > \ref cmdPref) of each other --
the same distance the editor itself uses to decide whether two endpoints
are close enough to connect. Each row gives <b>Track A</b> and <b>Track
B</b> with each one's own layer (<b>Layer #</b>/<b>Layer Name</b>), and
the <b>Gap</b> between them in inches. A pair like this usually means a
missed connection rather than two deliberate dead ends -- but check both
sides' layers first: on a multi-level layout, two endpoints can sit at
the same X/Y position in the shared 2D drawing while actually being on
two different physical levels, in which case the "gap" isn't a real one.

![](reportgaps.png)

A summary line above the list gives the number of near-miss pairs found,
how many open endpoints have no nearby partner (<b>isolated</b>), and how
many endpoints were excluded because they belong to a turntable, updated
whenever the report is generated or refreshed.

Turntable stall endpoints are always open by design and are excluded from
this report's pairing analysis entirely -- they're counted in the summary
line but never listed as a gap. This is a deliberate difference from the
Unconnected Endpoints Report (\ref cmdReports), which lists every open
endpoint including turntable stalls.

Isolated open endpoints (no nearby partner) are not listed in the
interactive table -- they're counted in the summary line, and listed in
full in the Save/Print output.

Unlike the Turnout Density Report (\ref cmdReportsturnout) and the other
phase-2 reports, this report always covers the whole layout and is
interactive, the same way the Unconnected Endpoints Report (\ref
cmdReports) is: selecting a row pans the <b>Main Canvas</b> (\ref mainW)
to the midpoint between the pair and draws a temporary open-circle
indicator there. The indicator is purely visual -- it's never saved with
the layout and disappears as soon as the report window closes. Its color
can be changed on the <b>Colors</b> (\ref cmdRgbcolor) dialog, under
<b>Report Indicator</b>.

The <b>Refresh</b> button recomputes the report in place -- useful after
fixing a flagged gap elsewhere in the layout, without closing and
reopening the report window.

The <b>Save As...</b> button opens a <i>File Save</i> dialog window that's
used to name and save the report to a text file.

The <b>Print</b> button opens the <i>Print</i> dialog which is used to
print the report.

The <b>Done</b> button closes the <b>Gaps Report</b> window.

*/

/**
\page cmdReportskinked Kinked Joints Report

The <b>Kinked Joints Report</b> command lists already-connected track
joints whose alignment has drifted out of the layout's Connection Angle
preference (Options > \ref cmdPref) since they were connected -- a joint
that would fail the editor's own alignment check today if it were
reconnected fresh. This usually means a track was moved or stretched
without re-validating its connection, or the layout file was edited or
imported from a source that didn't enforce the same tolerance. Each row
gives <b>Track A</b> and <b>Track B</b> with each one's own layer
(<b>Layer #</b>/<b>Layer Name</b>), and the angle discontinuity between
them in degrees.

![](reportkinked.png)

A summary line above the list gives the number of kinked joints found and
the Connection Angle tolerance they were checked against, updated
whenever the report is generated or refreshed.

Unlike the Turnout Density Report (\ref cmdReportsturnout) and the other
phase-2 reports, this report always covers the whole layout and is
interactive: selecting a row pans the <b>Main Canvas</b> (\ref mainW) to
the joint's position and draws a temporary open-circle indicator there,
the same way the Unconnected Endpoints Report (\ref cmdReports) and Gaps
Report (\ref cmdReportsgaps) do. The indicator is purely visual -- it's
never saved with the layout and disappears as soon as the report window
closes. Its color can be changed on the <b>Colors</b> (\ref cmdRgbcolor)
dialog, under <b>Report Indicator</b>.

The <b>Refresh</b> button recomputes the report in place -- useful after
fixing a flagged joint elsewhere in the layout, without closing and
reopening the report window.

The <b>Save As...</b> button opens a <i>File Save</i> dialog window that's
used to name and save the report to a text file.

The <b>Print</b> button opens the <i>Print</i> dialog which is used to
print the report.

The <b>Done</b> button closes the <b>Kinked Joints Report</b> window.

*/
